# Background work (WorkManager, Doze, standby buckets and exact alarms)

Sibling of [android-app](android-app.mdc), [ci-cost](ci-cost.mdc) and
[release-build](release-build.mdc). On-device verification is the
[android-deploy](../../skills/android-deploy/SKILL.md) skill.

This rule exists because the failure mode is **nothing at all**. No crash, no log line, no
exception — the worker simply runs less often than the code says, and the code keeps reading
correct forever. Nobody debugs a feature that looks like it works.

## The interval you ask for is a request, not a promise

`PeriodicWorkRequest` sets a floor of 15 minutes, and that is the *least* of it. Android
puts every app in a **standby bucket** and rations its jobs accordingly. The bucket is not
a detail you can reason about from the code — you have to ask the phone.

Verified from the platform source (`android/app/usage/UsageStatsManager.java`, android-37.0
— lower number means fewer restrictions):

```
STANDBY_BUCKET_EXEMPTED    =  5
STANDBY_BUCKET_ACTIVE      = 10
STANDBY_BUCKET_WORKING_SET = 20
STANDBY_BUCKET_FREQUENT    = 30
STANDBY_BUCKET_RARE        = 40
STANDBY_BUCKET_RESTRICTED  = 45
STANDBY_BUCKET_NEVER       = 50
```

Measured on a real phone (Android 15) after a fresh install of an app scheduled to sync
every 30 minutes:

```
$ adb shell am get-standby-bucket com.example.app
40                                    # RARE - jobs rationed to a handful a day
$ adb shell dumpsys deviceidle whitelist | grep com.example.app
                                      # nothing: not exempt from Doze
```

A newly installed app that has barely been opened is *exactly* the app the system considers
idle. So "every 30 minutes" was, in practice, a couple of runs a day. For anything whose
whole purpose is timeliness — a notification that something was posted — that is the same
as not working.

## Ask for the exemption, and only while it is missing

```kotlin
fun isUnrestricted(context: Context): Boolean {
    val power = context.getSystemService(PowerManager::class.java) ?: return true
    return power.isIgnoringBatteryOptimizations(context.packageName)
}

@SuppressLint("BatteryLife")            // see justification below
fun requestUnrestricted(context: Context) {
    val direct = Intent(
        Settings.ACTION_REQUEST_IGNORE_BATTERY_OPTIMIZATIONS,
        Uri.fromParts("package", context.packageName, null),
    )
    // Not every ROM ships the dialog; fall back rather than doing nothing.
    if (start(context, direct)) return
    if (start(context, Intent(Settings.ACTION_IGNORE_BATTERY_OPTIMIZATION_SETTINGS))) return
    openAppSettings(context)
}
```

with `<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.REQUEST_IGNORE_BATTERY_OPTIMIZATIONS" />`
in the manifest — without it the intent does nothing.

Granting it moves the app out of the rationed buckets entirely. Same phone, same app,
immediately after:

```
$ adb shell dumpsys deviceidle whitelist | grep com.example.app
user,com.example.app,10489
$ adb shell am get-standby-bucket com.example.app
5                                     # EXEMPTED
```

Rules for asking:

- **Only while the exemption is missing.** Read `isIgnoringBatteryOptimizations` and show
  the prompt from that, so granting it removes the prompt permanently. Never a launch-time
  dialog fired unconditionally.
- **Re-read on resume.** The user returns from a system dialog, not from a callback;
  `LifecycleEventEffect(Lifecycle.Event.ON_RESUME)` is enough. Without it the warning sits
  there after being satisfied, which teaches people to ignore it.
- **`BatteryLife` lint is suppressible only with a reason.** The check exists to stop Play
  apps asking casually. An internal or sideloaded app whose only function is timely
  notification has a real answer; write it in the suppression comment.
- Grant it without tapping when testing:
  `adb shell dumpsys deviceidle whitelist +com.example.app` (`-` to remove) — it writes the
  same `user` list the dialog does.

## The exemption does not cover the OEM's own restriction

The Doze whitelist is AOSP. Several skins add a **separate** per-app control that it does
not touch — MIUI/HyperOS "Autostart" is the common one — and an app can be battery-exempt
and still have its background work killed. There is no API for it and adb cannot set it, so
the only honest handling is to send the user to the page that has it:

```kotlin
Intent(Settings.ACTION_APPLICATION_DETAILS_SETTINGS,
       Uri.fromParts("package", context.packageName, null))
```

Offer it as a second action next to the exemption prompt, and say in the UI that it is
separate. Do not claim the app is unrestricted just because the AOSP check passes.

## Read what the phone decided; do not infer it from the code

The schedule you registered and the schedule the system will honour are different facts.
`dumpsys jobscheduler` holds the second one, and it answers in seconds — no waiting for a
period to elapse:

```
$ adb shell dumpsys jobscheduler | grep -A20 com.example.app
  JOB #u0a489/3: com.example.app/androidx.work.impl.background.systemjob.SystemJobService
      Requires: charging=false batteryNotLow=false deviceIdle=false
    Satisfied constraints:   CONNECTIVITY FLEXIBILITY DEVICE_NOT_DOZING WITHIN_QUOTA
    Unsatisfied constraints: TIMING_DELAY
    Enqueue time: -28m15s527ms
    Run time: earliest=+1m44s419ms, latest=none
```

How to read it:

- **The job exists at all** — if `grep` finds nothing, `enqueueUniquePeriodicWork` never
  landed, and no amount of waiting will help.
- **`Enqueue time` + `Run time`** together give the real period: enqueued 28m ago, next run
  in 1m44s, so this is a live 30-minute cycle that has already completed at least one lap.
  This is the proof the loop is running, and it is available immediately.
- **`Unsatisfied: TIMING_DELAY` alone is healthy** — it means "waiting for its window".
  Anything else unsatisfied (`CONNECTIVITY`, `WITHIN_QUOTA`) is the actual reason it is not
  running, and `WITHIN_QUOTA` in particular points straight back at the standby bucket.

Do not verify this by launching the app: opening it usually triggers a foreground refresh,
which overwrites the very timestamp you were about to inspect.

## Design so that a deferred run is harmless

Even exempt, Doze and quotas mean a run can be late. Anything that depends on the schedule
being punctual is already broken.

- **Derive "what is new" from persisted state, not from "since the last run".** Keep a set
  of keys you have already seen and diff against it. A run that is two hours late then
  reports the same set; a run that never happened loses nothing.
- **Seed silently on the first successful run.** On a fresh install every item on the server
  is "new". Measured in one case: roughly 350 notifications on first sync. Record the keys,
  notify nothing, set a `seeded` flag.
- **If the seen-set and the seeded flag can disagree, that is a notification flood waiting
  to happen.** Keys built from an enum name are one way it happens under R8 — see
  [release-build](release-build.mdc).
- **Bound the work per run.** Cap secondary fetches (detail pages, per-item requests) so one
  refresh cannot turn into ninety and get killed halfway.
- **Put an active-hours check inside the worker**, returning success early, rather than
  trying to express it in the schedule. A periodic request has no calendar, and a worker
  that returns early costs nothing.
- **One notification per group, not per item.** A batch that arrives at once — a day of
  homework, a backlog after a late run — must collapse, or a single event is indistinguishable
  from spam.

## Exact alarms (AlarmManager): a user-set time

WorkManager is for "about every N minutes". When the user picked the time (a reminder, a
scheduled send), use `AlarmManager.setExactAndAllowWhileIdle`, and know where it is denied.

- **Android 14+ denies `SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM` by default** for a fresh install of an app
  targeting 33+. Code that calls `setExact*` and falls back on `SecurityException` will
  quietly fall back to an inexact alarm on every new phone.
- **`USE_EXACT_ALARM` (API 33+) is granted at install and cannot be revoked.** Declare it,
  and keep `SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM` only for API 31 and 32 with `android:maxSdkVersion="32"`.
  Verified Oct-30-2026 (`flash-sms`, Nothing A009P, Android 16): `dumpsys package` showed
  `USE_EXACT_ALARM: granted=true` before the app asked for anything, and `dumpsys alarm` read
  `exactAllowReason=policy_permission` on the scheduled alarm. **Caveat (Play policy, not
  measured):** Google Play allows `USE_EXACT_ALARM` only for apps whose core function is an
  alarm, timer or calendar-like schedule; a sideloaded tool is fine, a Play app must justify it.
- **Still check `canScheduleExactAlarms()`** below 33 and offer the settings screen; fall back
  to `setAndAllowWhileIdle` rather than failing.
- **Alarms do not survive a reboot.** Store the schedule yourself and re-arm from
  `BOOT_COMPLETED`. Re-arm on `MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED` and on app open too: both are cheap, and
  they cover an update and a force-stop without having to prove what each one clears.
  (A force-stop test by counting `dumpsys alarm` entries was **inconclusive**: that dump
  includes recent history, so the counts are not live alarms. Do not cite such a count.)
- **Decide what a missed alarm means.** Firing a reminder or message hours late is worse
  than reporting the miss. `flash-sms` sends one missed by up to 6 hours on re-arm and marks
  an older one failed with a notification.
- **Make "fire" and "cancel" claim the item atomically.** The alarm and a user's cancel can
  land together; both should move the item out of PENDING with one locked compare-and-set,
  so exactly one wins and a late cancel says so instead of claiming success.

## Verify
- [ ] `adb shell am get-standby-bucket <pkg>` — know the number, and know that a fresh
      install sits in 40 (RARE) until the app is used.
- [ ] `adb shell dumpsys jobscheduler | grep -A20 <pkg>` shows the job, with `TIMING_DELAY`
      as the only unsatisfied constraint.
- [ ] The exemption prompt appears only while `isIgnoringBatteryOptimizations` is false, and
      clears on resume after granting.
- [ ] The manifest declares `REQUEST_IGNORE_BATTERY_OPTIMIZATIONS`, and the `BatteryLife`
      suppression carries a reason.
- [ ] The first successful run seeds without notifying.
- [ ] A late run produces the same result as a punctual one.
- [ ] Exact alarms: `dumpsys package <pkg> | grep USE_EXACT_ALARM` reads `granted=true`, and
      `dumpsys alarm` shows the alarm with an `exactAllowReason`; re-armed after a reboot.
